# SRG-APP-000223-WSR-000145  -  CAT II
# The web server must accept only system-generated session identifiers.
#
# Generated from the benchmark and the assessment determinations, then kept
# by hand. Edit this file to change how this requirement is assessed here;
# it is read at run time. Regenerating refreshes the benchmark text in place
# and leaves the criteria, the determination and any verification block as
# they are.

id:              SRG-APP-000223-WSR-000145
group:           V-206398
severity:        medium
cat:             CAT II
determination:   APPLICABLE
grade:           C
decidable:       machine
confidence:      documented
cci:             CCI-001664
control_rev5:    SC-23

contribution:    Splunk Web issues and tracks its own session identifiers through its
                 session subsystem, and the only route by which an externally supplied
                 identity can be accepted is the trusted-header SSO path. The product can
                 close that route: strict SSO mode confines SSO requests to trusted source
                 addresses, the trusted IP list must be agreed in both server.conf and
                 web.conf, and exact matching of the remote user header prevents a client
                 from substituting a look-alike header name.

governs:         web.conf - Configures the Splunk Web interface.
check:           web.conf [settings] tools.sessions.on equals true
check:           web.conf [settings] enableSplunkWebSSL in 1|true


# --- vendor verification: generated, do not edit by hand
vendor:          web.conf [settings] SSOMode https://help.splunk.com/en/splunk-enterprise/administer/manage-users-and-security/10.4/authenticate-into-splunk-enterprise-using-hardware/configure-splunk-enterprise-to-use-a-common-access-card-for-authentication
vendor:          web.conf [settings] allowSsoWithoutChangingServerConf https://help.splunk.com/en/splunk-enterprise/administer/admin-manual/10.4/configuration-file-reference/10.4.0-configuration-file-reference/web.conf
vendor:          web.conf [settings] remoteUserMatchExact https://help.splunk.com/en/splunk-enterprise/administer/admin-manual/10.4/configuration-file-reference/10.4.0-configuration-file-reference/web.conf
vendor:          web.conf [settings] tools.sessions.on https://help.splunk.com/en/splunk-enterprise/administer/admin-manual/10.4/configuration-file-reference/10.4.0-configuration-file-reference/web.conf
retired_check:   web.conf [settings] tools.sessions.on equals true
                 reason: The spec states the setting as a literal rather than a type:
                         "tools.sessions.on = true. Whether or not user session support is
                         enabled. Always set this to true." No default is stated.
retired_check:   web.conf [settings] SSOMode not_equals permissive
                 reason: 10.4.2 spec: "SSOMode = [permissive | strict]... When set to
                         'permissive': Requests to Splunk Web that originate from an
                         untrusted IP address are redirected to a login page where they can
                         log into Splunk Web without using SSO. When set to 'strict': All
                         requests to Splunk Web will be restricted to those originating from
                         a trusted IP except those to endpoints that do not require
                         authentication. Default: lax" - 'lax' is not one of its own two
                         values. The vendor 10.4 web.conf page states the default is strict.
                         The 10.4 CAC page states SSOMode "must use permissive" for CAC
                         authentication, with trustedIP set to 127.0.0.1.
retired_check:   web.conf [settings] allowSsoWithoutChangingServerConf not_equals 1
                 reason: "Whether or not to allow SSO without setting the 'trustedIP'
                         setting in server.conf as well as in web.conf. If set to 1, enables
                         web-based SSO without a 'trustedIP' setting configured in
                         server.conf. Default: 0"
# --- end vendor verification

external:        Stripping of client-supplied identity headers by the authenticating proxy
                 before forwarding - reverse proxy configuration
external:        The contents of the trusted IP list - organization-defined
external:        Session identifier generation itself, which is a fixed property of the
                 product's session subsystem and has no configuration surface - Splunk
                 Enterprise implementation
external:        Exact matching of the user name in a trusted header, which web.conf
                 [settings] remoteUserMatchExact governs only where header-based single
                 sign-on is configured through server.conf [general] trustedIP; with no
                 trusted address set the setting is inert and cannot carry the objective.

citation:        web.conf.spec:920-922 (tools.sessions.on = true, "Always set this to
                 true"); web.conf.spec:787-795 (SSOMode = [permissive|strict], default
                 strict; strict restricts all requests to those originating from a trusted
                 IP); web.conf.spec:806-811 (allowSsoWithoutChangingServerConf = [0|1],
                 default 0; 1 enables SSO without a trustedIP setting in server.conf);
                 web.conf.spec:767-775 (remoteUserMatchExact = [0|1], default 0, "Set to 1
                 when you set up SSO"); web.conf.spec:728-734 (remoteUser, with the spec's
                 own caution on the treatment of HTTP headers); web.conf.spec:977-983
                 (tools.sessions.storage_type = [file])
finding:         The application does not confine itself to system-generated session
                 identifiers to the extent the product allows: session support is not
                 enabled, SSO is in permissive mode, SSO is permitted without the agreed
                 trusted IP setting in server.conf, or exact matching of the remote user
                 header is not enforced.
